("jig puts each mesh's triangles in a uniform grid of cells, walks each edge of the other mesh in steps of half a cell, tests the triangles in the cells it passes, and sets the gap to 0 when any edge crosses. The peg and gear now report `≈ 0.000 mm` and `asm.interference`, which engine test 42 checks.\n\nThe fix has limits. The crossing test runs only when some vertex already lies within $2c + 1$ mm of the other mesh ($c$ the clearance, so 1.8 mm by default): a 2 × 2 mm pin pushed straight through a gear's web, far from every vertex of the gear and with its own vertices 7 mm away, still reports `∞ mm`, and so does a small cube buried entirely inside the gear, since no surfaces cross at all. A complete check would run the crossing test whenever the boxes overlap and add a point-in-mesh test, a ray cast whose crossings are counted, for containment. Coplanar triangles are left to the distance pass. When a pair matters, `{ exact: true }` settles it on the B-rep, at about a quarter of a second for the peg and gear.",
 "jig puts each mesh's triangles in a uniform grid of cells, walks each edge of the other mesh in steps of half a cell, tests the triangles in the cells it passes, and sets the gap to 0 when any edge crosses. The peg and gear now report `≈ 0.000 mm` and `asm.interference`, which engine test 42 checks.\n\nThe crossing test runs for every pair that reaches the mesh path, not only when a vertex comes near: a 2 × 2 mm pin pushed straight through the web of a 30-tooth gear has every vertex of either mesh at least 7 mm from the other, and it is found by its long edges crossing the gear's side faces. One solid wholly inside the other crosses nothing, so a last test casts a ray from one vertex of each mesh, slightly skewed so that it misses edges and vertices, and counts the triangles of the other mesh it crosses: an odd count means inside. A 2 mm cube buried in the gear's web is found that way. Both report `≈ 0.000 mm` and `asm.interference`, as `{ exact: true }` does. Coplanar triangles are left to the distance pass, which sees them at distance 0. For the peg and gear the exact check takes about a third of a second."),
